==== exit ====
0
==== stdout ====
raphael help output - Text results, JSON envelopes, paging and diagnostics.

Standard output holds results only. Without --json, lists, search and favorites
print one row per item: id, type, state, path and title, in columns padded to
the widest value on the page. State is -, active, archived or active,archived,
and comes before the title, which is always last. A header line is printed only
on a terminal. A write prints a line naming the item, then its path, and its
revision when it has one; a write that changed nothing says Unchanged; get
prints every field, including the update time. List order defaults to slug
ascending; use `list --order-by updatedAt:desc` for recent changes. Search
orders by relevance and does not accept --order-by. Bodies are printed as
Markdown by default.

With --json, a successful command prints exactly one document:
{"ok":true,"command":"get",...data}. Full-item reads and writes use entity;
move, archive and restore use node; lists and favorites use items; search uses
items[].node. These are machine field names. Items include path and updatedAt,
an ISO 8601 UTC time such as 2026-10-19T10:42:07.123Z; text output prints the
same string. No createdAt field is exposed. Update, move, archive, restore,
favorite and unfavorite include changed; false means success without a change
and without a revision bump. Move and update also report previousPath, the path
before the change; text output prints it as was: only when the path changed.
Favorite commands report `nodeId`, `isFavorite` and `node`, which is null when
unfavorite names an id that does not exist. Path reports path.

Paged results include page with skip, limit, hasMore, nextSkip and
archivedLeftOut, which is true when archived items were left out of the answer.
The result footer and runnable next-page command go to standard error, never
standard output. Empty-result notes, warnings, hints, prompts and failures also
go to standard error. Warnings appear in warnings[] on JSON successes as
{code, message}, plus constructs for markdown_degraded. Codes:
durability_unconfirmed: login saved the key, but the filesystem did not confirm
the write is durable. environment_overrides: login saved the key, but
RAPHAEL_ENDPOINT or RAPHAEL_API_KEY is set: with both, remote commands use them
instead; with one alone, remote commands refuse to run. environment_still_set:
logout removed the saved login, but RAPHAEL_ENDPOINT or RAPHAEL_API_KEY is still
set: with both, remote commands use them; with one alone, remote commands refuse
to run. version_skew: status found a server of another release that speaks the
same protocol. markdown_degraded: create or update did not store some Markdown
as written; constructs lists them by name. Closing a pipe early does not turn a
completed command into a failure.

JSON failures leave stdout empty and print exactly one document to stderr:
{"ok":false,"command":"get","error":{...}}. The only other text stderr can then
hold is for a person at a terminal: login's prompts, and the note that --body @-
is reading from one. command is the command as typed (help for every help form),
or null when none was recognized. error always has code, category, message,
hint, exitCode, applied and retryable. Optional fields include status, field,
target, serverMessage, details, and connection {endpoint, source, configFile}
once a connection was resolved. category is the word for exitCode: failed for 1,
usage for 2, not_found for 3, conflict for 4, not_configured for 5, retryable
for 6, outcome_unknown for 7, interrupted for 130 and 143. code is a server code
or one of this CLI's: usage, cancelled, interrupted, invalid_request,
unexpected, not_configured, unsupported_platform, no_home,
relative_xdg_config_home, relative_home, directory_unusable,
directory_uncreatable, file_unusable, file_unreadable, file_too_large,
file_malformed, write_failed, incomplete_environment, malformed,
unsupported_scheme, credentials_in_url, query_not_allowed, fragment_not_allowed,
api_key_missing, api_key_unsendable, timeout_out_of_range, unreachable,
blocked_port, not_http, connection_lost, timeout, incompatible_protocol,
not_raphael, bad_response, http_status, port_in_use, listen_denied,
host_unavailable, listen_failed, database_in_use, database_newer,
database_unusable, start_failed, option_invalid, config_unreadable,
config_too_large, config_malformed, config_unsafe, config_invalid,
env_unreadable, env_too_large; `raphael help exit-codes` gives each one's exit
codes and meaning. For a server's answer, details carries what the server
reported, each field optional: field, index, reason, limit, currentRevision,
blocking, parentType, childType, location, element; blocking is {id, type}. For
incompatible_protocol, details is
{serverProtocol, serverVersion, cliProtocol, cliVersion}, with null for what the
server did not say, and serverCode when its answer carried a code of its own.
applied is false when no change was applied, true when known applied, and null
when unknown. Retry decisions must consider exitCode and applied; do not blindly
retry an uncertain write. The JSON envelope and exit-code meanings are stable
within a major version.

help --json prints one document to stdout: {"ok":true,"command":"help",...}. For
the root page and for reference it has commands, topics, examples, exitCodes and
errorCodes; for a command, definition; for a topic, topic. A definition has
name, summary, group, writes, usage, behavior, options, examples, prints and
next, and may have constraints and seeAlso. Each option has name, type and
description, and may have short, multiple, whole {min, max}, valueName, default
and constraint. A topic has name, summary, body and examples. Each errorCodes
entry has code, source, exitCodes and categories, and a meaning for this CLI's
codes.

For text write failures, the state line distinguishes Nothing was changed., The
change was not sent., and The change may or may not have been applied. Set
RAPHAEL_DEBUG=1 to include diagnostic stacks. Keep stdout for results when
scripting.

On standard error, a line that starts with hint: is a runnable next step, and a
line that starts with warning: is something a success did not do as asked. Other
lines are information only, such as Showing 1-3. or Nothing changed: it was
already archived. A note that also suggests a command puts that command on its
own hint: line. A hint that is a command quotes all of it, as in hint: Run
"raphael favorites" to see your favorites. or hint: Did you mean
"raphael list /work"?

Archive causes print one per line, nearest first: directly, or through the
archived container above the item, then who set the cause in parentheses. (user)
is a cause set with archive; restore removes only that cause, and only on the
item itself. JSON carries each cause as origin, owner and reason.

The default page contains at most 50 results. --limit accepts 1–500; --skip
skips results. At most 10 paths or ids may be searched or listed in one request.

For list: --order-by accepts slug, updatedAt, id with asc or desc; for example
`--order-by updatedAt:desc`. Search orders by relevance and does not accept
--order-by.

Examples:
  raphael list / --json
  raphael list / --json --order-by updatedAt:desc
  raphael get --id 42 --json --return-format tiptap

Next: raphael help reference
